.TH SAPHIRA-MKINITRAMFS 8 "September 2026" "Saphira Linux" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
saphira-mkinitramfs \- build, inspect and unpack a Saphira initramfs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B saphira-mkinitramfs
.B build
.BI \-\-kernel " VERSION"
.BI \-\-root " DIR"
.BI \-\-output " FILE"
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B saphira-mkinitramfs
.B list
.I ARCHIVE
.br
.B saphira-mkinitramfs
.B unpack
.I ARCHIVE
.I DESTINATION
.br
.B saphira-mkinitramfs
.B verify
.I ARCHIVE
.RI [ \-\-kernel " VERSION" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Builds a reproducible, zstd-compressed cpio
.B newc
initramfs containing a small static
.B /init
and whatever the configuration asks for.
.PP
One generator serves two callers. The media builder produces the
.I installer
initramfs from the installer root; the installer produces the destination
machine's initramfs from the root it has just populated. Neither has separate
tooling.
.SH THE KERNEL ARGUMENT IS MANDATORY
.B \-\-kernel
is required and is never inferred from
.BR uname \-r .
The installer runs inside its own environment, where the running kernel is the
installer's kernel and not the kernel of the machine being installed, so a
default would silently produce an initramfs for the wrong kernel.
.SH HOOKS ARE OF TWO KINDS
The two kinds are kept in different directories because they run at different
times on different machines.
.TP
.B Build-time hooks
.IR /usr/lib/saphira/initramfs/build-hooks.d/NN\-name
run on the BUILDING system while the archive is constructed, are not shipped
in it, and may use build-time software. They may add files to the staging tree.
.TP
.B Runtime hooks
.IR /lib/saphira/initramfs/early.d/NN\-name
and the same layout under
.IR root.d/
and
.IR pre-switch-root.d/
are copied INTO the initramfs and executed by
.B /init
at boot, in lexical order within each stage. A failing hook stops the boot
visibly.
.PP
A runtime hook is an executable, not necessarily a script. When one is a
script, the generator includes its shebang interpreter and that interpreter's
shared-library closure automatically, so the base initramfs does not require a
shell for its own sake.
.SH WHAT THE GENERATOR DOES
For each requested binary it resolves the ELF interpreter and the whole
recursive
.B DT_NEEDED
closure. For each requested module it takes the dependency and softdep closure
from that kernel's
.BR modules.dep " and " modules.softdep .
For each script hook it takes the interpreter. Members are sorted, ownership is
normalised to 0:0, timestamps are pinned from
.BR SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH ,
and the result is compressed with a fixed zstd level, so an unchanged input
tree produces a byte-identical archive.
.SH MODES
The initramfs configuration selects one of two terminal modes.
.TP
.B mode=switch
Hooks prepare
.IR /newroot ;
.B /init
verifies that a new root exists and has an executable init, then
.BR switch_root s
into it. This is the installed-system path.
.TP
.B mode=ram
.B /init
executes the configured installer entrypoint from the initramfs itself and
never leaves it. The installer payload is a complete environment in RAM and
has no
.IR /newroot .
The interface exists so the installer initramfs does not require
.B /init
to be redesigned.
.SH FAILURE
The output is replaced atomically and only after a complete archive has been
built and verified. A failed build therefore leaves the previous initramfs
exactly as it was. Refuse rather than emit a partial payload: an initramfs that
is subtly incomplete fails at boot with no diagnostic.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/saphira/initramfs.conf
default contents
.TP
.I /usr/lib/saphira/initramfs/init
the packaged static
.B /init
.TP
.I /usr/lib/saphira/initramfs/build-hooks.d/
build-time hooks
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/saphira/elf-closure
ELF closure helper; the reason binutils is not a runtime dependency
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SAPHIRA_TMPDIR
scratch location (default
.IR /build/tmp ).
Host
.I /tmp
is never used.
.TP
.B SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
timestamp pinning for reproducible archives.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR saphira-profiles (5)
